   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.5",
    "topic_title": "Cellular Respiration",
    "stem": "Isolated mitochondria are supplied with pyruvate, ADP, and phosphate. When oxygen is removed from the solution, NADH accumulates inside the mitochondria and ATP synthesis by ATP synthase drops nearly to zero. Which explanation best accounts for these observations?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Oxygen is a reactant that ATP synthase combines with ADP and phosphate to form ATP, so without oxygen ATP synthase lacks a substrate.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the misconception that oxygen makes ATP directly. ATP synthase joins ADP and phosphate using energy from protons flowing down their gradient; oxygen never enters that reaction and acts only at the end of the chain, accepting electrons. Fix: Ask which molecule each step actually uses, and trace oxygen to where it accepts electrons and forms water."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Without oxygen to accept electrons at the end of the electron transport chain, electron flow stops, so protons are no longer pumped, the proton gradient dissipates, and ATP synthase has no gradient to drive ATP synthesis.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Oxygen is the final electron acceptor. When it is absent, electrons back up in the chain, so NADH cannot hand off its electrons and accumulates. With no electron flow, no protons are pumped into the intermembrane space, so chemiosmosis through ATP synthase stops."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Without oxygen, glycolysis cannot run, so no pyruvate or NADH is produced to feed the electron transport chain.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses which steps need oxygen. Glycolysis does not require oxygen and continues anaerobically through fermentation; it also occurs in the cytosol, not in isolated mitochondria already given pyruvate. The accumulating NADH shows electrons are being supplied but not passed on. Fix: Check where each stage occurs and whether it directly consumes oxygen before blaming it for a stall."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Oxygen is needed to break the carbon-carbon bonds of pyruvate, so without it no carbon is oxidized and no electrons are released to NADH.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats respiration like burning, with oxygen attacking the fuel directly. In the Krebs cycle, carbon is released as CO₂ and electrons are transferred to NAD⁺ and FAD without oxygen reacting with the fuel. The observed NADH buildup shows carbon oxidation is still loading carriers. Fix: Track where electrons go at each step and ask whether the data show carriers being reduced."
     }
    ]
   },
